The canonical routing principle is:
Appropriate Intelligence > Maximum Intelligence
The most capable model is not necessarily the correct model.
The correct model is the model or capability that satisfies the request while respecting the applicable constraints.
The Router must therefore avoid unnecessary escalation.
A simple request should not automatically require the most expensive or remote intelligence resource.
A privacy-sensitive request should not automatically be sent to a remote provider.
A local capability should be preferred where it adequately satisfies the request and applicable constraints.
A specialized capability should be preferred when it provides a materially better match for the intent.
The Router must consider privacy before routing information to an external intelligence provider.
Privacy is a routing constraint.
