The Router may use a routing score to compare candidate capabilities.
The canonical conceptual scoring model is:
Score = (Privacy × 3) + (Capability Match × 3) + (Context Match × 2) + (Latency × 2) + (Cost × 1)
This score is a routing mechanism, not a constitutional value.
The weights may evolve through validated architectural decisions.
The Principle and higher-level contracts remain superior to any scoring formula.
A numerical score must never authorize an action that violates a higher-priority constraint.
For example:
A provider with excellent latency and capability match must not be selected if its use violates a privacy requirement.
A cheaper capability must not be selected if it cannot satisfy the required task.
A highly capable model must not be selected if it violates a local-only policy.
Routing is constrained optimization, not unrestricted optimization.
